'''''Dombeya burgessiae''''', the '''rosemound''', is a widespread species of flowering plant in the family [[Malvaceae]].<ref name="RHS_84232" >{{cite web |title=''Dombeya burgessiae'' Rosemound |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/84232/i-dombeya-burgessiae-i/details |date=2022 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=10 September 2022 |quote=Other common names; Wedding Flower [3] }}</ref> It is native to seasonally dry areas of tropical Africa, and has been introduced to Pakistan, [[Assam]], and Trinidad and Tobago.<ref name="POWO_822960-1" >{{cite POWO |id=822960-1 |title=''Dombeya burgessiae'' Gerrard ex Harv. |access-date=10 September 2022 }}</ref> A variable shrub or multi-stemmed tree from {{cvt|2|to|8|m|ft|0}} tall, it is used for its fiber (for ropes and baskets), wood (bows and tool handles), its edible pith, and for friction sticks to make fire.<ref name="UTP" >{{cite web |url=https://tropical.theferns.info/viewtropical.php?id=Dombeya+burgessiae |title=''Dombeya burgessiae'' Gerrard ex Harv. Malvaceae |last1=Fern |first1=Ken |date=20 July 2022 |website=tropical.theferns.info |publisher=Tropical Plants Database |access-date=10 September 2022 }}</ref> It is occasionally planted as an ornamental.<ref name="UTP" />
